 ### Design concept
 
-The provider treats the process tree as the unit of lifetime. POSIX children spawn detached (their own process group) so the whole tree is signalled by negative group id with a direct-child fallback; Windows terminates by root pid through `taskkill /T`. Signalling, escalation, and teardown guard on tree liveness rather than direct-child settlement, so a TERM-trapping helper cannot outlive the handle unnoticed.
+The provider treats the process tree as the unit of lifetime. POSIX children spawn detached (their own process group) so the whole tree is signalled by negative group id with a direct-child fallback; Windows terminates by root pid through `taskkill /T`. Windows ordinary children hide their console window so a GUI or no-console host does not flash a shell window. Window hiding does not change the spec's stdio dispositions. Signalling, escalation, and teardown guard on tree liveness rather than direct-child settlement, so a TERM-trapping helper cannot outlive the handle unnoticed.
 
